<section class="panel">
	<div class="hero">
		<div class="left">
			<div class="eyebrow">POCO X3 NFC &middot; postmarketOS &middot; Plasma Mobile</div>
			<h1>Real Linux on your POCO X3 NFC.</h1>
			<p class="lede">Flash one image and you get a working phone: three
			cameras, calls, LTE, GPS, sensors, both speakers, 120&nbsp;Hz. Then
			<code>apk upgrade</code> keeps it current, kernel included.</p>
			<div class="btns">
				<a class="btn ghost" href="devices.html">See what does not work</a>
				<a class="btn ghost" href="install.html">Other ways to install</a>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="right">
			<a class="btn" href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases/latest/download/@IMG@.xz"
			   style="text-align:center">Download the image &mdash; 1.2 GB</a>
			<div class="os">
				<input type="radio" name="os" id="os-linux" checked>
				<input type="radio" name="os" id="os-win">
				<input type="radio" name="os" id="os-mac">
				<div class="tabs">
					<label for="os-linux">Linux</label>
					<label for="os-win">Windows</label>
					<label for="os-mac">macOS</label>
				</div>
				<div class="pane pane-linux"><div class="sh"><pre><i># install fastboot:</i>
<i>#   Debian/Ubuntu  apt install android-sdk-platform-tools</i>
<i>#   Arch           pacman -S android-tools</i>
<i>#   Fedora         dnf install android-tools</i>

<i># phone OFF, hold volume-down + power until it says</i>
<i># "fastboot", then plug the USB cable in</i>
sudo fastboot devices      <b># must print your phone</b>

xz -d @IMG@.xz
sudo fastboot flash userdata @IMG@
sudo fastboot reboot</pre></div></div>
				<div class="pane pane-win"><div class="sh"><pre><i># 1. platform-tools.zip from developer.android.com,</i>
<i>#    unzip it, open PowerShell in that folder</i>
<i># 2. the Google USB Driver, or Windows will not see</i>
<i>#    the phone in fastboot</i>
<i># 3. 7-Zip to unpack the .img.xz</i>

<i># phone OFF, hold volume-down + power, plug the cable</i>
.\fastboot devices

.\fastboot flash userdata @IMG@
.\fastboot reboot
<b># not tested by me: reports welcome</b></pre></div></div>
				<div class="pane pane-mac"><div class="sh"><pre>brew install android-platform-tools xz

<i># phone OFF, hold volume-down + power until it says</i>
<i># "fastboot", then plug the USB cable in</i>
fastboot devices           <b># no sudo needed on macOS</b>

xz -d @IMG@.xz
fastboot flash userdata @IMG@
fastboot reboot
<b># not tested by me: reports welcome</b></pre></div></div>
			</div>
			<div><b>First time on this phone?</b>Two things come before this: Xiaomi
			has to unlock the bootloader (a Mi account and seven days of waiting), and
			U-Boot goes on <code>boot</code> &mdash; the build for your panel, Tianma
			or Huaxing. <a href="install-pmos.html">Both, step by step</a>.</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>What is in the image</h2></div>
		<p>One file, 1.2&nbsp;GB compressed. Nothing to add afterwards.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>The hardware</th><td>Patched kernel, cameras, in-call audio,
			sensors, GPS, power-off, charge mode, 60 and 120&nbsp;Hz.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Plasma Mobile, fixed</th><td>A dialpad that does not drop digits,
			quick settings for location, power saving and USB power, a screen that turns
			itself off, notification sounds.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Six own apps</th><td>Navigation with voice, screenshots on the
			buttons, always-alive apps, battery per app, smart unlock,
			find-my-phone.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>The usual ones</th><td>Firefox at 120&nbsp;Hz, mail and calendar,
			two-factor, Mastodon, Matrix, RSS, photo gallery.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top:20px">
			<a class="btn" href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases/latest/download/@IMG@.xz">Download the image &mdash; 1.2 GB</a>
			<a class="btn ghost" href="install.html">Other ways to install</a>
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>What you actually get</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Take photos</th><td>All three cameras, with autofocus: 64&nbsp;Mpx
			rear, front, ultra-wide.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Make calls</th><td>Voice both ways over LTE. The other end hears an
			echo. Still open.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Hear things</th><td>Both speakers, earpiece, and the volume you left
			it at. Out of the box this phone booted mute.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Rotate the screen</th><td>Accelerometer, light, compass, proximity
			, all four.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Find yourself</th><td>Twelve satellites, fix in about 40&nbsp;s from
			cold. postmarketOS ships the GPS locked; this unlocks it.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Save battery</th><td>60&nbsp;Hz instead of 120 when you ask, and a
			charge screen when you plug it in switched off.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Turn it off</th><td>And it turns off. That took a kernel patch.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Use it as a phone</th><td>Navigation with voice, screenshots with
			the buttons, apps that survive the task switcher, battery per app, smart
			unlock, find-my-phone without Google.
			<a href="packages.html">All six &rarr;</a></td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>03</span><h2>Questions you are about to ask</h2></div>
		<div class="grid quiet">
			<div>
				<div class="t">Can I use it as my only phone?</div>
				<p>Someone does, since August 2026. Calls, data, camera and GPS work. You
				lose the fingerprint reader and you get an echo on calls.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Can I go back to Android?</div>
				<p>Yes. Flash a stock ROM over <code>userdata</code> and reflash the
				stock <code>boot</code>. Nothing here is irreversible.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Will an update break it?</div>
				<p>The patched packages are forks with their own names, so an upstream
				release cannot silently replace them. The kernel comes with updates, so
				reboot deliberately, not in a hurry.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Is the image encrypted?</div>
				<p>No, and you should not want one that is: the master key would be born
				on my machine. <a href="install-encrypted.html">Build your own &rarr;</a></p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Do I need to trust your binaries?</div>
				<p>Not much: everything is built from public recipes and signed. Or build
				it yourself with <code>scripts/build --all</code>.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Why is the fingerprint impossible?</div>
				<p>The sensor works. Its bus belongs to the TrustZone and is never handed
				to Linux. Four routes measured, four dead ends.
				<a href="devices.html">The detail &rarr;</a></p>
			</div>
		</div>
